          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Dirichlet L-functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\chi
            are a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-function"} L-function
            induced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+characters"} Dirichlet characters
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L_\chi(s) \coloneqq \underoverset{n=1}{\infty}{\sum} \chi(n)n^{-s}
                  \\,.
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GoldfeldHundley11"} Goldfeld-Hundley 11 (2.2.1)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Continuations"} Continuations
          %p
            The completion of this by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gamma+function"} Gamma function
            factor (and a power of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            and of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductor"} conductor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mellin+transform"} Mellin transform
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+theta+function"} Dirichlet theta function
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Continuations"} Continuations, p. 8
          %p
            By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+reciprocity"} Artin reciprocity
            Dirichlet L-function are equal to suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+L-functions"} Artin L-functions
            induced by 1-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representations"} Galois representations
          %p
            In terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adelic+integral"} adelic integral
            representation of L-functions via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Iwasawa-Tate+theory"} Iwasawa-Tate theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+character"} Dirichlet character
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            then the corresponding Dirichlet L-functions are simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adelic+integrals"} adelic integrals
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Garrett11"} Garrett 11, section 2.2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi 𝕀
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  s \mapsto \int_{\mathbb{I}} \chi(x) \;f(x)\; {\vert x\vert}^s
          %p
            for suitable Schwartz functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idele+group"} idele group
          %p
            Hence Dirichlet L-functions are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mellin+transforms"} Mellin transforms
            of suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+functions"} theta functions
            (“theta kernels”), see. e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#Stopple"}> Stopple, p. 3
            ).
          %p
            Where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+characters"} Dirichlet characters
            (see there) are essentially
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+forms"} automorphic forms
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idele+group"} idele group
            (hence for
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            is the concept of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+L-function"} automorphic L-function
          %p
            The differential geometric analog of a Dirichlet L-function is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+function"} eta function
            (see there for more) of a differential operator.
